---
title: Home
---
<div class="section-header">
  <h1>Sierra example</h1>
  <span class="text-sm text-muted">one schema, both halves</span>
</div>

<!-- Prose stays on one line per sentence on purpose: Mesa joins the lines of a
     static text node without reinserting the newline as a space, so a wrapped
     paragraph renders as "…and its400s from it". See the note in the README. -->
<p class="text-body">
  One <code>db/schema.lite</code>. The API derives its tables, its 401s and its 400s from it; the browser derives make() defaults, field rules, enum options, relation targets and gate levels from the same file. Neither side restates the other.
</p>

<!-- .tiles is the responsive grid: auto-fit + minmax, no media query here.
     The button sits in a .cluster because .stack stretches its children,
     and a button stretched to the card's width is a banner, not a button. -->
<div class="tiles" style="--tile-min: 18rem">
  <article class="card stack">
    <h2 class="h4">Leads</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">
      A list, with a delete button the schema decides whether to offer — <code>@@gate</code> wants level 5, so <code>can('delete', level)</code> is asked before it renders.
    </p>
    <div class="cluster"><a class="btn outlined" href="/leads/">Open the list</a></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card stack">
    <h2 class="h4">New lead</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted">
      A form generated entirely from the schema — no field list, no types, no enum values and no mention of the accounts service in the component.
    </p>
    <div class="cluster"><a class="btn outlined" href="/leads/create/">Open the form</a></div>
  </article>
</div>

<article class="alert info">
  <div class="alert-content">
    Sign in from the header to get past <code>@@gate("0.4.4.5")</code> — reads are public, writes want level 4, delete wants 5.
  </div>
</article>
